To make the background translucent blur, you can wrap your VStack in a VisualEffectView like this:

```swift
VisualEffectView(effect: NSVisualEffectView.BlurEffect(style: .extraLight)) {
    VStack {
        // your existing VStack code
    }
}
```

To set the window size, you can modify your ContentView struct to specify a custom window size:

```swift 
struct ContentView: View {

    var body: some View {
        // your VStack code
    }
}

struct ContentView_Previews: PreviewProvider {
    static var previews: some View {
        ContentView()
            .frame(width: 960, height: 960)
    }
}
```

The key things:

- Wrap VStack in VisualEffectView for blur 
- Specify .frame(width: 960, height: 960) on the ContentView preview struct

This will make a 960x960 window with a translucent blur background. Let me know if any part of that is unclear!
Ah I see, if you are setting the frame on the ContentView inside the WindowGroup, that won't actually update the window size. To set the window size itself, you need to specify it on the WindowGroup:

```swift
@main
struct Daily48App: App {
    var body: some Scene {
        WindowGroup {
            ContentView()
        }
        .frame(width: 333, height: 333)
    }
}
```

By putting the `.frame` modifier on the WindowGroup rather than the ContentView, it will actually apply that sizing to the window itself.

A couple other notes on this:

- You may want to also set `.fixedSize()` on the WindowGroup to disable resizing
- On Mac, the minimum window size is more like 500x500 - 333x333 may get adjusted upward

But hopefully setting the frame on the WindowGroup gets you the window resizing you were looking for! Let me know if any other issues come up.
Good catch! The `WindowGroup` returns a scene, not a view, so you can't directly apply view modifiers like `.frame` to it.

Instead, you can set the window size by getting a reference to the window and setting its minSize and maxSize:

```swift
@main
struct Daily48App: App {
    var body: some Scene {
        WindowGroup {
            ContentView()
        }
        .windowStyle(.hiddenTitleBar)
        .windowToolbarStyle(.unified)
        .windowResizability(.contentSize)
        .windowMinSize(CGSize(width: 333, height: 333))
        .windowMaxSize(CGSize(width: 333, height: 333))
    }
}
```

The key things here:

- Use `.windowMinSize()` and `.windowMaxSize()` to set the size
- Include `.windowResizability(.contentSize)` to disable user resizing

This will force the window to be 333x333 and not allow resizing.

Let me know if that helps or if any other questions come up!
The issue here is that you are creating the NSWindow programmatically in your AppDelegate, but also declaring a SwiftUI WindowGroup in your Scene. So you end up with two windows - the NSWindow and the WindowGroup window.

To fix this, you should pick one approach. Since you are already using SwiftUI for your ContentView, I would recommend removing the AppDelegate code and just using the WindowGroup.

Here is how you could set up a single window with the desired behavior in SwiftUI:

```swift
@main
struct Daily48App: App {

    var body: some Scene {
        WindowGroup {
            ContentView()
        }
        .windowStyle(.hiddenTitleBar)
        .windowToolbarStyle(.unified) 
        .windowResizability(.contentSize)
        .windowMinSize(CGSize(width: 333, height: 333))
        .windowMaxSize(CGSize(width: 333, height: 333))
    }
}
```

The key things:

- Removed the AppDelegate and its window creation code
- Use WindowGroup with modifiers:
    - `.windowMinSize()` and `.windowMaxSize()` to set the size
    - `.windowResizability(.contentSize)` to disable resizing

This will give you a single 333x333 window created in SwiftUI, without the duplicate from the AppDelegate.

Let me know if that helps explain the two windows or if any other questions come up!
That error is occurring because in your WindowGroup, you have:

```
.commands {
    CommandGroup(replacing: .newItem) {} 
}
```

The `.commands` modifier can only be applied to scenes that are embed in a `DocumentGroup`. Since you just have a plain `WindowGroup`, using `.commands` will result in that error.

To fix it, just remove the `.commands` modifier:

```swift
@main
struct Daily48App: App {

    var body: some Scene {
        WindowGroup {
            ContentView() 
        }
        .windowStyle(.hiddenTitleBar)
        .windowToolbarStyle(.unified)
        .windowResizability(.contentSize)
        .windowMinSize(CGSize(width: 333, height: 333))
        .windowMaxSize(CGSize(width: 333, height: 333))
    }
}
```

The `.commands` modifier is only needed if you want to customize keyboard shortcuts or menu items, but for a simple single window app like this, it is not needed.

Let me know if that helps explain the error!
Ahh I see, the issue here is that `EmptyScene` is not actually a valid `Scene` since it returns an `EmptyView`. A `Scene` needs to return a `WindowGroup`, `DocumentGroup`, etc.

Since you are manually creating the NSWindow in your AppDelegate, you don't need a SwiftUI scene at all. You can just use an empty app struct:

```swift 
@main
struct Daily48App: App {
    @NSApplicationDelegateAdaptor(AppDelegate.self) var appDelegate

    var body: some Scene {
        // No scene needed
    }
}
```

Then all the window management will happen in your AppDelegate.

Essentially:

- Remove the EmptyScene struct 
- Return no scene from your App struct
- Keep the AppDelegate window creation code

This avoids that error about EmptyScene not conforming to Scene, since you no longer need a SwiftUI scene at all when creating the window manually.

Let me know if that helps explain the latest error!
You can make the title bar transparent by setting the titlebarAppearsTransparent property on the NSWindow to true:

```swift
func applicationDidFinishLaunching(_ notification: Notification) {
    window = NSWindow(/*...*/)

    window.titlebarAppearsTransparent = true
}
```

This will make the title bar blend into whatever content is behind it, giving that transparent look.

A couple other related options:

- Set `.titleVisibility = .hidden` to hide the title bar completely 
- Use `.windowStyle(.hiddenTitleBar)` to hide title bar and integrate controls into toolbar instead

So for completely transparent look:

```
window.titlebarAppearsTransparent = true
window.titleVisibility = .hidden
```

And don't forget to set `window.isOpaque = false` to allow transparency to show through.

Let me know if that helps achieve the transparent title bar effect you were going for!
Ah I see - to get that fully transparent title bar effect, you need to:

1. Set the window's background color to clear:

```swift 
window.backgroundColor = .clear
```

2. Enable full size content view:

```swift
styleMask: [.fullSizeContentView] 
```

3. Set the titlebar appearance specifically:  

```swift 
window.titlebarAppearsTransparent = true
window.titleVisibility = .hidden
```

The key things:

- Clear backgroundColor 
- .fullSizeContentView style 
- Hide actual title 

This will make the title bar blend into the content behind it, giving that transparent glass appearance.

One other tweak - you may want to set the window frame a bit larger than the content, like:

```swift
window = NSWindow(contentRect: .init(x: 0, y: 0, width: 256, height: 768))
```

This will make space for the hidden titlebar controls.

Let me know if that helps achieve the fully transparent title bar look!

