Here is a C# .NET application that prints "Hello" from 10 different threads:

```csharp
using System;
using System.Threading;

namespace ThreadingExample
{
    class Program
    {
        static void Main(string[] args)
        {
            for (int i = 0; i < 10; i++)
            {
                Thread thread = new Thread(new ThreadStart(PrintHello));
                thread.Start();
            }
        }

        static void PrintHello()
        {
            Console.WriteLine("Hello from thread {0}", Thread.CurrentThread.ManagedThreadId);
        }
    }
}
```

This code:

- Creates 10 Thread objects, each with a PrintHello delegate that prints a message
- Starts each thread, which calls the PrintHello method
- The PrintHello method prints "Hello from thread" along with the thread ID
- So you'll see 10 "Hello from thread" messages printed, each from a different thread

